Personal access tokens
=======================

Overview
========

A personal access token (PAT) is a second way for a user to authenticate
against the REST API, alongside the existing per-user API key ("API access
key" on the My account page).

The legacy API key has three properties that make it awkward from a security
point of view: there is exactly one per user, it never expires, and once it
leaks it stays valid until the user notices and resets it. Personal access
tokens address this:

* a user can create any number of tokens, each with its own name, so a
  token can be scoped to a purpose (e.g. "CI pipeline", "laptop backup
  script") and identified later;
* every token has an expiration date, capped by an administrator-configured
  maximum lifetime;
* a token can be revoked on its own, without touching the account's other
  tokens or its legacy API key.

A token authenticates exactly like the legacy API key: it identifies the
same Redmine user, with the same permissions, on the same REST API. It does
not introduce a separate, more restricted, kind of access.

Token format and storage
=========================

A token's plaintext value has the form:

    redmine_pat_<40 alphanumeric characters>

i.e. the prefix "redmine_pat_" (defined as PersonalAccessToken::PREFIX)
followed by 40 characters generated with SecureRandom.alphanumeric, for
about 238 bits of entropy (40 characters from a 62-character alphabet).
This shape is also how Redmine tells a personal access token apart from a
legacy API key or a real username at authentication time
(PersonalAccessToken.token_format?).

Redmine never stores the plaintext value. On creation
(app/models/personal_access_token.rb) it stores:

* token_digest: the SHA256 hex digest of the plaintext, under a unique
  database index (see the migration below), used to look the token up;
* token_last_chars: the last 4 characters of the plaintext, kept only so
  the token list can show something recognizable
  (PersonalAccessToken#masked_value renders it as "redmine_pat_" followed
  by an ellipsis and those last 4 characters).

The plaintext is held in memory only, in the token's plaintext_token
attribute, and is shown exactly once: on the page displayed right after
creation (app/views/personal_access_tokens/created.html.erb). That
response is marked no-store so it is not cached. If a token's value is
lost, there is no way to recover it; the only option is to delete the
token and create a new one.

The database table is created by
db/migrate/20260922120000_create_personal_access_tokens.rb, with
user_id, name, token_digest (unique index), token_last_chars, expires_on,
created_on and updated_on columns.

Using a token
=============

A personal access token can be used with any of the three transports the
legacy API key already supports. In the examples below, replace
"redmine_pat_<40 chars>" with an actual token value.

1. The X-Redmine-API-Key HTTP header (recommended, see "Security notes"
   below):

     curl -H "X-Redmine-API-Key: redmine_pat_<40 chars>" \
       https://example.com/issues.json

2. The "key" request parameter:

     curl "https://example.com/issues.json?key=redmine_pat_<40 chars>"

3. HTTP Basic authentication, with the token as the username and any
   value (even an empty one) as the password:

     curl -u redmine_pat_<40 chars>:x https://example.com/issues.json

The header transport is recommended because, unlike the "key" parameter,
it never appears in the request URL; see "Limitations and notes" below for
what that exposure means in practice.

A personal access token reaches everything the legacy API key reaches:
every REST API endpoint, and every other API-enabled action that accepts
key-based authentication, including Atom feeds, CSV exports and PDF
exports.

Managing tokens
================

My account (any user)
----------------------

Signed-in users manage their own tokens from My account, under "Personal
access tokens" (this link, and the whole feature, is only shown while
"Enable REST API" is on):

* the list at /my/personal_access_tokens shows each token's name, masked
  value, creation date and expiration date, and marks expired tokens;
* "New personal access token" (/my/personal_access_tokens/new) asks for a
  name and an expiration date. The date picker is bounded: it cannot be
  today or earlier, and it cannot be later than the configured maximum
  lifetime from today. The field is pre-filled with 30 days from today, or
  the maximum lifetime if that is shorter;
* creating or deleting a token requires sudo mode, i.e. re-entering the
  account's password, wherever sudo mode is enabled (see "Limitations and
  notes" below — it is off by default);
* the plaintext value is shown exactly once, right after creation, with a
  "copy" button.

Administration (administrators)
--------------------------------

Administration -> Personal access tokens (also only shown while the REST
API is enabled) lists every token of every user:

* it can be filtered by status ("active" or "expired") and by user (a
  substring match against login, name or email, the same matching used by
  the user autocomplete elsewhere in Redmine);
* an administrator can delete any user's token; deleting one requires
  sudo mode (again, only where sudo mode is enabled);
* the token's owner receives an email notification when their token is
  deleted this way, just as they do when they delete it themselves.

Administration -> Settings -> API
-----------------------------------

The API settings tab has a new "Maximum lifetime of personal access
tokens" field (setting name personal_access_token_max_lifetime, in days,
default 90). It bounds how far in the future any user's token expiration
date can be set; see "Limitations and notes" for what happens with 0 or a
negative value, and for existing tokens when this is lowered.

The same tab holds the settings of the API audit log, which records every
REST API request and the credential used; see doc/API_AUDIT_LOG.

Security model
===============

The design relies on the following, reader-facing, guarantees:

1. Only a token's SHA256 digest is stored. Nobody, including a Redmine
   administrator with database access, can recover a token's plaintext
   from the database; a lost token can only be replaced.
2. A token is only usable while its owning user account is active. A
   locked or otherwise inactive owner makes every one of that user's
   tokens stop authenticating immediately, with no separate action
   needed (PersonalAccessToken.authenticate checks token.user&.active?).
3. Unknown tokens, expired tokens and tokens whose owner is locked all
   produce the same 401 response, so a caller cannot use the response to
   tell these cases apart.
4. Personal access tokens cannot be used to manage personal access
   tokens: the token management pages (both under My account and under
   Administration) only accept the browser (HTML) format, not the REST
   API's .json/.xml formats, and creating or deleting a token additionally
   requires sudo mode, which is tied to an authenticated web session with
   a recently re-entered password, not to a bearer credential.
5. A request authenticated with a personal access token never discloses
   the account's legacy API key, on the "My account" API response or on
   a user's "show" API response (User#authorized_by_personal_access_token?
   suppresses api_key on both).
6. A request authenticated with a personal access token cannot change
   the account's own email address through My account (PUT /my/account);
   the request is rejected with a validation error instead.
   This closes off using a leaked token to redirect password-reset email
   to an attacker-controlled address.
7. Redmine emails the token's owner whenever one of their tokens is
   created or deleted, whether they or an administrator did it. The
   notification names the token, and states who performed the action and
   from which IP address.
8. A token-shaped value is stripped out of Redmine's own request logging
   wherever Redmine's code controls it: request parameters named "key" are
   filtered out of the Rails log (config/application.rb), and a
   personal-access-token-shaped "key" parameter is removed from the URL
   Redmine redirects an anonymous request to when asking it to log in, so
   it does not end up in that redirect, its log line, or the login form.

Backward compatibility
=======================

This feature is purely additive. Legacy API keys, OAuth (Doorkeeper)
tokens, and HTTP Basic authentication with a real username and password
all keep working exactly as before; none of their code paths were
changed. Existing scripts and integrations that use the legacy API key
require no changes, and a personal access token can be used as a
drop-in replacement for the API key in any of them: the same header, the
same "key" parameter, and the same use as an HTTP Basic username all
work.

Limitations and notes
======================

* A token passed with "?key=" appears in the request URL. That means it
  can end up in the web server's access log, in browser history, and, if
  the requested page is HTML and contains outgoing links or images, in
  the Referer header sent to those other destinations; the page's own
  links carry the token in the URL too. The X-Redmine-API-Key header
  avoids all of this. This is exactly how the legacy API key already
  behaves when passed the same way; personal access tokens do not make it
  worse or better.
* Sudo mode is disabled by default in Redmine (see the commented-out
  "sudo_mode" setting in config/configuration.yml.example). The sudo-mode
  protection on creating and deleting tokens (both under My account and
  under Administration) only has an effect where an administrator has
  turned sudo mode on.
* A token issued to an administrator carries full administrator power,
  including the ability to create other administrator accounts, so an
  administrator's own tokens should be given a short lifetime. In
  particular, changing another user's email address through
  PUT /users/:id is not guarded against personal-access-token
  authentication the way My account's own email change is; an
  administrator's token can still be used to change any user's email.
  The same request can also set any user's password directly
  (UsersController#update assigns params[:user][:password] whenever it
  is present, and UsersController declares accept_api_auth for
  :update), which is a shorter path to a full session than the
  email-change / password-reset detour described in "Security model"
  above.
* A personal access token does not itself satisfy two-factor
  authentication. It is only ever issued from a web session that has
  already passed 2FA (where enabled) and sudo mode (where enabled); the
  token does not re-prove either of those on each API request.
* Setting the maximum lifetime (personal_access_token_max_lifetime) to 0
  or to a negative number silently falls back to the built-in default of
  90 days (PersonalAccessToken.max_lifetime); it is not treated as "no
  limit".
* While the REST API is disabled (Administration -> Settings -> API ->
  "Enable REST API"), personal access tokens do not authenticate
  anything, and the token management pages, both under My account and
  under Administration, respond with 403.
* Lowering the maximum lifetime only limits new tokens' expiration
  dates; it does not shorten or invalidate tokens that already exist with
  a later expiration date.
* An expired token is not deleted automatically; it stays in its owner's
  list (marked as expired) and in the administration list until someone
  deletes it.
* A token's name must be unique within its owner's tokens, but this is
  enforced only by model validation (validates_uniqueness_of), not by a
  database index, so it relies on the application, not the database, to
  prevent a race from creating two same-named tokens for one user.
* Only English translations were added, in config/locales/en.yml; other
  languages are expected to be translated afterwards, following Redmine's
  usual translation workflow.

Known issues found in existing code (pre-existing, out of scope)
==================================================================

While building this feature, a few issues were found in code this feature
touches but did not introduce. They are called out here so they are not
mistaken for part of this feature, and are left unfixed as out of scope
for this change:

* app/views/mailer/security_notification.html.erb crashes if a caller
  passes a :url without also passing a :title: its "elsif @url" branch
  calls "link_to @url" with a single argument, which is not a valid way
  to call link_to for a plain URL. This feature works around it by always
  passing both :url and :title when it calls
  Mailer.deliver_security_notification.
* app/views/my/account.api.rsb discloses the account's api_key to
  OAuth-authenticated requests, not only to session-authenticated ones;
  only the personal-access-token case was fixed here
  (see "Security model" above). By contrast, app/views/users/show.api.rsb
  already excluded OAuth-authenticated requests from seeing their own
  api_key before this change; this feature only added the equivalent
  personal-access-token exclusion there.
* An OAuth access token whose owner has been locked raises a
  NoMethodError in app/controllers/application_controller.rb: the code
  path calls User.active.find_by_id, which returns nil for a locked
  user, and then calls oauth_scope= on that nil.
* When the legacy API key is sent as the HTTP Basic username, it is
  still passed through User.try_to_login before falling back to
  User.find_by_api_key. That means it can reach a configured
  authentication source and can be written to the debug SQL log, the way
  any login attempt can. Personal access tokens skip try_to_login
  entirely (they are recognized by their shape and never treated as a
  login name), so they cannot reach an auth source or that log.

