Manual smoke test
------------------

Happy path:

1. Administration -> Settings -> API: check "Enable REST API" and save.
2. Log in as a regular user, go to My account, and open "Personal access
   tokens".
3. Click "New personal access token", enter a name and an expiration
   date within the allowed range, and submit. If sudo mode is enabled
   (see "Limitations and notes"), confirm your password first.
4. Copy the plaintext value shown on the confirmation page (it has the
   form redmine_pat_<40 chars> and is shown only this once).
5. From a terminal, confirm it authenticates over the header:
   curl -i -H "X-Redmine-API-Key: redmine_pat_<40 chars>" \
     http://localhost:3000/users/current.json
   Expect a 200 response for the same user, with no api_key field.
6. Confirm the other two transports work the same way:
   curl -i -u redmine_pat_<40 chars>:x http://localhost:3000/users/current.json
   curl -i "http://localhost:3000/users/current.json?key=redmine_pat_<40 chars>"
7. Delete the token from My account -> Personal access tokens (or, as an
   administrator, from Administration -> Personal access tokens) and
   confirm the owner receives an email for both the creation and the
   deletion (the automated tests above already assert exactly one mail is
   sent for each; for a live check, configure SMTP delivery as described
   in doc/INSTALL and watch the recipient's mailbox).

Negative path, from the same setup:

1. Expired token: in `bundle exec rails console`, since expires_on is a
   read-only attribute after creation (attr_readonly), force it directly
   with update_all rather than update/save:
     token = PersonalAccessToken.find_by(:token_last_chars => '<last 4
       chars from step 4 above>')
     PersonalAccessToken.where(:id => token.id).update_all(:expires_on =>
       1.day.ago)
   Retry the curl call from step 5; expect 401.
2. Deleted token: delete the same token from the UI, then retry the same
   curl call with the same plaintext value; expect 401.
3. Locked owner: create a fresh token, note its plaintext, then in the
   console lock its owner: User.find_by_login('<login>').lock! ; retry
   the curl call; expect 401. Unlock it again with
   User.find_by_login('<login>').activate! to restore normal access.
4. REST API disabled: Administration -> Settings -> API, uncheck "Enable
   REST API" and save. Retry the curl call; expect 403 (the API rejects
   every credential while it is disabled). Also visit
   /my/personal_access_tokens and /admin/personal_access_tokens in the
   browser; expect 403 on both. Re-enable the setting afterwards.
5. Another user's token id: the ownership scope on destroy
   (User.current.personal_access_tokens.find_by(:id => ...) in
   PersonalAccessTokensController#find_personal_access_token) is covered
   by the automated test
   test_destroy_foreign_token_should_respond_with_404 in
   test/functional/personal_access_tokens_controller_test.rb; this is not
   practical to exercise by hand: an HTML DELETE from curl has no session
   and no CSRF token, so it is rejected with 422 before the scoped lookup
   runs, the .json form 404s from routing (the route is declared with
   :format => false) rather than from the ownership scope, and a
   non-admin has no way to see a foreign token's id in the UI in the
   first place. To try it manually anyway, get a foreign id from
   `bundle exec rails console`
   (User.find_by_login('<other login>').personal_access_tokens.first.id),
   then, signed in as the first user with My account -> Personal access
   tokens open (so a valid CSRF token is on the page) and sudo mode off,
   use the browser's console to fire the DELETE with that token, e.g.:
     fetch('/my/personal_access_tokens/<foreign id>', {method: 'DELETE',
       headers: {'X-CSRF-Token':
         document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]').content}})
   Expect 404, not 403.
6. "?key=" filtering in the log: with the REST API enabled again, run
     curl "http://localhost:3000/issues.json?key=redmine_pat_<40 chars>"
   then check log/development.log for that request's "Started GET" and
   "Parameters:" lines. Both should show "[FILTERED]" in place of the
   token value, and the plaintext value should not appear anywhere else
   in the log file.
